{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788286279,"nonce":"MOt/7T57QjvvmlMleEOyteClx40=","message":"THE MOTIVATING CASE, FROM THE RUN THAT MET M2 (session 18) — and one of this\nissue's open questions is now settled by measurement rather than by argument.\n\n## What happened, and why it is this issue's evidence\n\nM2 placed `templates/bedroom.ir.json` as 22 blueprints and needed to advance\nuntil colonists had finished building it. There is no condition for that, so I\nadvanced 2000 ticks, looked, and advanced 6000 more. Dorian caught the first\nnumber and asked whether it was part of the instructions. It was not — it was a\nguess, and there was nothing better available.\n\nThat is the failure this issue describes, in its most ordinary possible form.\nNot \"stop when food gets low\" on a colony being driven into starvation — just\n\"stop when the thing I asked for is done\", on the single most common action an\nagent will ever take. Every shipped matcher is a journal tap, and a finished\nbuilding emits no letter, no threat and no alert.\n\n**Evan's framing, 2026-09-01, and the answer:** he asked whether the useful thing\nis a trigger (\"when the building is done\") or \"just a building progress shell you\ncan look at\". It is the trigger, and the reason is structural rather than\naesthetic. A progress shell cannot help an agent that has no way to sleep: every\nlook is a protocol round trip against a 0.25-1 s floor, and BETWEEN two looks the\nagent must still guess a tick count — so a shell hands back the same arbitrary\nnumber this issue exists to remove, plus latency. The halting decision belongs\ninside the tick loop. (A layout-scoped read is still wanted, for a different\nreason, and is filed as `36999fd`.)\n\n## THE EDGE QUESTION IS NOT THEORETICAL — this case is false-at-start-shaped\n\nThe Open questions ask \"whether a false-at-start predicate should halt\nimmediately or require an edge (the second is probably right)\". Session 15\nrecorded the same thing for the clock (`hour \u003e= 6` is true all afternoon).\n\nConstruction makes it concrete and makes it the DEFAULT rather than an edge case.\nThe natural spelling of \"wait until the build is done\" is\n`construction.frames == 0` — and on the run above that predicate was TRUE at the\nmoment of the call, three times over:\n\n- true before `place-layout` was ever sent (empty map, no frames);\n- true for the ~900 ticks between placement and the first blueprint becoming a\n  frame, because blueprints awaiting materials are not frames;\n- true again at the end, which is the only one meant.\n\nSo a same-tick evaluation halts instantly and reports success on a room that does\nnot exist. **Requiring an edge is not enough either** — the second case is a real\nfalse-to-true-to-false-to-true sequence, and an edge detector halts on the wrong\ncrossing. What this predicate actually needs is a scope that is monotone: \"every\nplacement in `ly-1` is resolved\", where a placement is resolved when it is built\nor cancelled and never goes back. `Placements.Answer` already computes exactly\nthat per id (session 16: completion is an absence and the id survives it).\n\nThat is a stronger claim than the Open questions currently make, and it points at\na design consequence: **some predicates are not expressible as an operator over a\ndigest path at all.** `construction.frames == 0` is a digest path and it is the\nWRONG predicate; the right one is a question about a transaction. If the surface\nis only `path op value`, the most-wanted condition in the mod cannot be written.\n\n## Suggested addition to this spec's surface, not a separate issue\n\nA named predicate family beside the path form — `until:{layout:\"ly-1\"}`, halting\nwhen every placement in that layout is resolved, reporting which ids ended\n`built` and which `cancelled`. It composes with `timeout_ticks` unchanged, and a\nlayout that can never finish (material unreachable) exits on timeout with the\nper-placement states as the diagnosis, which is a strictly better failure report\nthan the fixed-tick advance gives today.\n\n## Two acceptance criteria this case suggests\n\n- `advance {until:{layout:\"ly-N\"}}` halts within one cadence window of the last\n  element completing, on the M2 bedroom, and does NOT halt at t=0 despite\n  `construction.frames == 0` being true when the call is made — the false-at-\n  start case, run deliberately.\n- The same call against a layout whose material is unreachable exits on\n  `timeout_ticks` and reports the unresolved placements and their states, rather\n  than merely `reason: \"timeout\"`.\n\nCross-refs: `36999fd` (layout scope for `construction`, the observer half of the\nsame gap), `54b0c9a` (the shortfall count, found on the same run).","files":null}]}